<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Merapi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Merapi is an active volcano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, north of Yogyakarta, and one of the most active in the world. Its ash made the surrounding plains fertile and supported early Javanese kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a>, whose court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> in 929, possibly after eruptions. Major eruptions in modern times, including those of 1930 and 2010, have killed hundreds of people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-merapi.jpg" alt="Mount Merapi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fahmi Widayat · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pendidikan_Karakter_Dan_Nasionalisme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Merapi lies on the border between the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-region-of-yogyakarta">Special Region of Yogyakarta</a>, about 28 kilometres north of Yogyakarta city. Its name, in Javanese, means Fire Mountain, and it has erupted regularly since 1548. It is the youngest in a group of volcanoes in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, at a subduction zone where the Indo-Australian plate passes under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> plate. Villages stand on its flanks as high as 1,700 metres above sea level.</p><p>Eruptions in the area began about 400,000 years ago. Until about 10,000 years ago they were mostly effusive, with basaltic lava. Since then they have become more explosive, with viscous andesitic lavas that often build lava domes. The collapse of a dome produces pyroclastic flows, which the people of the region call wedhus gembel, shaggy goat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Small eruptions come every two to three years and larger ones every 10 to 15 years or so. Notable eruptions, often with many deaths, occurred in 1006, 1786, 1822, 1872 and 1930. The eruption of 1006 is claimed to have covered all of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> with ash. In 1930 pyroclastic flows destroyed thirteen villages and killed 1,400 people. On 22 November 1994 a pyroclastic flow killed 27 people, mostly in the town of Muntilan.</p><p>On 25 October 2010 Merapi erupted on its southern and south-eastern slopes. Over the next month 353 people died and 350,000 fled their homes, most of the damage coming from pyroclastic flows, with lahars after heavy rain on 4 November. The eruption lowered the summit by 38 metres to 2,930 metres. Seismic monitoring began on the mountain in 1924, and the volcano is one of the Decade Volcanoes chosen for special study. After 2010 a zone in nine villages in Cangkringan district was closed to permanent settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Merapi is important to the Javanese people, especially those living near its crater. Villagers hold that a spirit palace lies inside it, ruled by the armourers Empu Rama and Empu Permadi, a counterpart to the Yogyakarta Sultanate. In one widely told origin story the gods moved Mount Jamurdipo to the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> to balance the island and buried the two armourers, who had refused to leave their forge, beneath it.</p><p>For the Sultanate the mountain marks the northern end of a sacred axis running through the Tugu monument, the sultan's palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast. An area of 6,410 hectares around the mountain has been a national park since 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Merapi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
